WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.300
Function identity is one of the central controls in serverless security.

2
00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:14.280
Each function runs with some form of execution identity: a role, service account, managed identity, binding, or platform permission set.

3
00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:17.980
That identity defines what the function can do after the code starts.

4
00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:24.320
A function that only needs to read one object path should not be able to delete every storage bucket.

5
00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:27.860
A queue consumer should not automatically administer the whole account.

6
00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:34.280
A public API handler should not inherit the same authority as an administrative migration job.

7
00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:37.280
Least privilege starts with function purpose.

8
00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:43.540
Define what the function is supposed to do, then scope permissions to the exact resources and actions required.

9
00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:50.280
Avoid broad wildcard permissions, shared high-privilege roles, and one role reused across unrelated functions.

10
00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:57.820
Separate read, write, administrative, deployment, migration, and operational permissions where practical.

11
00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:05.120
A little extra role design work upfront can greatly reduce blast radius when a handler has a bug or a dependency behaves unexpectedly.

12
00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:08.920
Permissions should be reviewed when the workflow changes.

13
00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:19.500
New triggers, new storage paths, new database tables, new queues, new downstream APIs, and new deployment behavior can all change the function's required authority.

14
00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:23.280
Permissions that were once reasonable can become stale or excessive.

15
00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:30.820
Infrastructure-as-code review should make those changes visible, and reviewers should ask whether the permission still matches the function's actual purpose.

16
00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:35.740
Least privilege does not replace input validation or authorization.

17
00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:45.600
A function can have narrow cloud permissions and still make a bad business decision if it trusts a tenant ID from the request or skips object-level authorization.

18
00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:52.100
Think of function identity as the platform boundary and application authorization as the business boundary.

19
00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:55.400
Mature serverless security needs both.
